 docs/features/F37-focus-ring.md           | 14 ++++++---
 9 files changed, 105 insertions(+), 32 deletions(-)

diff --git a/collab/QUESTIONS.md b/collab/QUESTIONS.md
index 324bcc5f..c8260030 100644
--- a/collab/QUESTIONS.md
+++ b/collab/QUESTIONS.md
@@ -932,13 +932,21 @@ One line per product decision made while building, newest last.
 - F02: windows in a tile travel as the tile draws them (app colour and icon, at their place in the tile) and turn into themselves on the way. Out of view, a window looks as it does at the end where it can be seen.
 - F02: nothing fades in place except what has nowhere to go: a tab that becomes active during a camera move, a floating window growing out of the Desktop's tile, a window that was minimized while out of view.
 - F02: one full-display stage per display with a layer per window, instead of a panel per window. It needs a backdrop, because the real windows jump underneath: a picture of everything below the windows (wallpaper, icons, widgets) with Screen Recording permission, otherwise the wallpaper's file, otherwise dark grey; never clear.
-- F02: the stage draws every window Arbor can see on that display, also the ones it does not arrange and the ones that stay put, stacked as the window server stacks them. Windows it cannot see (dialogs, sheets, panels, picture-in-picture) are covered for the length of the motion.
-- F02: the stage sits one level above floating windows, below the Dock and the menu bar, and lets clicks through.
-- F02: a second command retargets the motion from where things are (the swarm's shared design decision) instead of finishing it (the spec). A mouse button going down anywhere finishes it (T42); a key press does not.
+- F02: the stage draws every window that moves, stacked as the window server stacks them. A window that takes no part in the motion (one that stays put, pinned or not, arranged or not; a dialog, a panel, a window of Arbor's own, which Arbor cannot even see) is not drawn: the stage has a hole there and the window itself is seen, live. The rule: only if nothing stacked in front of it crosses its frame at any moment, because through a hole one sees the real screen, where what moves has already arrived. Otherwise a window Arbor knows is drawn standing still, and one it does not know is covered for the length of the motion.
+- F02: three kinds of window only look as if the rule applied. One whose stand-in is still on its way from the motion before gets no hole (the real window arrived long ago). A stranger lying over a moving window of its own app is taken for a sheet or an attached dialog, which macOS carries along, and is covered: a hole where it was would show a piece of what is behind it now. A pinned window counts as frontmost even if something has just covered it, because Arbor raises it a moment later (F49).
+- F02: the stage draws a shadow of its own round every hole. The real one lies outside the window's frame, under the stage, and would go out and on again.
+- F02: the stage sits one level BELOW floating windows, so that what other apps keep there (picture-in-picture, a call's toolbar) and Arbor's own heads-up panels stay in sight above it. It lets clicks through. Like every overlay it is `.transient`, so Mission Control hides it.
+- F02: whoever takes a window out of view or brings one in by hand, without laying out (F53's summon), calls `putMotionRight(on:)`, and so do the reports of windows that are in no arrangement: if the display's stage is playing something, the picture is made again from what is to what is, which closes the hole over a window that has gone, opens one over a window that has come, and sends a stand-in to where its window really went. Only the picture: a layout would be an occasion for much else to be decided afresh (F61 made a tile of a window), and nothing the controller decides may depend on whether a stage is up.
+- F02: a window Arbor arranges whose app keeps it above all others ("always on top") is above the stage too. The real one is seen going to its place at once, which cannot be helped; it has no stand-in, which would glide along underneath it like a ghost, and it blocks no holes (`StackedWindow.floatsAbove`). No pictures are taken of windows above the normal level: none of them has a stand-in.
+- F02: with pictures off the backdrop of every motion is the wallpaper's FILE, so it lies as System Settings has it (Fill, Fit, Stretch, Centre: `WallpaperFit`, from `NSWorkspace.desktopImageOptions`) over the fill colour chosen there; a Desktop in a plain colour, a clear picture over that colour, came out dark grey. No cure without a picture of the screen: Tile, wallpapers that change with the time of day (the first picture of the file is taken), aerials and videos (`desktopImageURL` names a still).
+- F02: stand-ins and holes have the window radius everything of Arbor's follows (`ContinuousCorners.windowRadius`: 17 on macOS 26, 10 before) with the continuous curve.
+- F02: a second command retargets the motion from where things are (a design decision shared by every feature) instead of finishing it (the spec). A mouse button going down anywhere finishes it (T42); a key press does not. The controller remembers what each stage is playing for as long as it lasts: a window that was leaving goes on to where it was going (the new plan, made from the real windows, has nothing to say about one that is out of view before and after); one that was minimized or closed meanwhile fades away although nothing else has to move; and what is being played already is not asked for again, nor because the order of the windows changed.
+- F02: a motion that is corrected (an app had the last word on its frame) or put right (`putMotionRight`) takes what is left of its time, at least 0.15 s, and not the whole 0.46 s again: 0.61 s at most, as the polish rubric asks (`docs/VIABLE.md`, section 8). A second command is a new motion and takes its time.
+- F02: a window let go where macOS tiles it is about to be moved by macOS, with a motion of macOS's own. What the drop changes for the others is applied at once, without a stage: a stage would lie over that motion, with a hole where the window no longer is. And whatever else moves a window Arbor knows in mid-motion (its app, macOS, a hand) has the picture put right, as for windows Arbor does not arrange.
 - F02: the curve is a cubic Bézier (0.2, 0, 0, 1): an ease-out, somewhat stronger than Core Animation's own, without overshoot. `Transition.curve`, for anything else that moves.
 - F02: animated: camera moves, commands that change the arrangement, drops, releasing a window's edge, windows closing, minimizing, returning, docked by a rule. At once: gap drags, putting a moved window back, macOS Space switches, display changes, settings (also `exclude` and `include`), launch, pause, resume, quit, anything while the user holds a window, and whatever moves nothing by more than a point (so switching tabs is instant).
 - F02: `animationDuration` is kept between 0 and 2 s; a value that is not a number counts as 0.46.
-- F02: pictures are kept between motions instead of being discarded after each (the spec), because a motion cannot wait for one: taken at launch, 0.4 s after the focus moves, at the start of a motion and 0.3 s after it. In memory only, let go of when the window leaves the screen and when Arbor pauses or quits. At most 3 million pixels each (12 MB), so small windows are as sharp as the display and a full-screen one on a Retina display is a little softer (the brief said 1x; soft text the moment the stage goes up would give the trick away).
+- F02: pictures are kept between motions instead of being discarded after each (the spec), because a motion cannot wait for one: taken at launch, at the start of a motion and 0.3 s after it, and 0.4 s after a window that has none yet gets the keyboard (not at every change of focus: every picture is a moment of screen recording). The picture of the desktop, the largest, is taken again with a motion, and by housekeeping only when it is a minute old. Arbor's own windows below the normal level are left out of it. In memory only, let go of when the window leaves the screen and when Arbor pauses or quits. At most 3 million pixels each (12 MB), so small windows are as sharp as the display and a full-screen one on a Retina display is a little softer (the brief said 1x; soft text the moment the stage goes up would give the trick away).
 - F02: if an app does not do as it is told (refuses its slot, rounds its size), the controller sends a corrected transition right after moving the windows.
 - F02: `float-all` leaves bringing back windows that are out of view to the relayout, so that they fly in instead of appearing first. They end where they did before. Since F48, which decides where they go, `float` and `float-all` hand their moves to `relayout(…, puttingBack:)`: no real window moves before the stage is up.
 - F02: `ARBOR_NO_PICTURES=1` runs Arbor as it is without Screen Recording permission, for looking at that on a Mac that has it.
