+## How it is judged
+
+**What Linux proves** (`make check`): everything the controller decides. Which windows get a stand-in, from where to where, and which a
+hole (`TransitionTests`, `TransitionHolesTests`); that one action gives each window one frame and its stand-in ends exactly there, from rest
+and in mid-motion, with the motion on and off, in one motion (`oneActionMovesEachWindowOnceAndItsStandInEndsThere`); that a motion put right takes
+only what is left of its time (`aMotionThatIsPutRightTakesWhatIsLeftOfIt`); that the curve starts fast, settles gently and never overshoots; that no real window moves
+before the stage is asked for; that a day of random steps goes the same way with and without motion, frame for frame
+(`TransitionInvariantTests`: raise its three counts to 80, 300 and 200 after any change to the controller); when the ring flashes
+(`FocusRingFlashes`); that the macOS-only code type-checks (`make typecheck-mac`); and that the Mac script below says what it means
+(`scripts/mac-shell/rehearse.sh scripts/mac-shell/shots-F02.sh`).
+
+**What a Mac must run**: `scripts/mac-shell/run.sh scripts/mac-shell/shots-F02.sh`, once, unattended, about five minutes after the build. In this form it has
+been rehearsed on Linux and has not run on a Mac. First the
+look that ships (the app's colour and icon), slowly; what takes no part; 0.46 s with eight windows, one kept in view, one floating, and one Arbor
+knows nothing about (dock, frame, walk, show everything, close), light and dark, macOS's tiling on and off; then `windowPictures` on, allowed and refused; then the motion off. `swift test --filter StagePictureTests` draws the real
+stage off screen and leaves film strips in `.build/pictures/F02/`.
+
+**What is judged afterwards, and from which files**: `output.txt` (every ✔ and ✘, the `moves:` line of each trace, the `VERDICT` lines on pictures and
+crash reports); `traces/*.jsonl` (every frame given to a window and what it took, over time: two frames for one window in one act
+are a jolt); `arbor*.log` (`stage up`, `retargeted`, `open in n place(s)`, `down`; `pictures: took …`, `none, …`); the films `NN-*-1…7.png`, which alone
+show whether a hole fits its window, whether anything hops when the stage lifts, and whether anything flickers.
+
+**Only a Mac can say**: whether the pinned window shows live through its hole, with fitting corners and a steady shadow, or blinks there between
+the moment a command brings the framed window to the front and the moment Arbor raises the pinned one again (the trace has both times); whether floating
+panels and Arbor's own stay above a stage at level 2; whether a click and a key in mid-motion reach the real window; whether
+the drawn wallpaper lies on the real one; whether anything hops at the hand-over; whether a mask over the whole display costs frames with eight
+windows; whether Mission Control, a Space switch or ⌘Tab in mid-motion leave anything behind; whether pictures can be had without a crash.
